tivities, such as work, community service, a work program or half-time education, to reach the 80-hour monthly minimum."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What earnings level satisfies the Medicaid community engagement requirement without tracking hours?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Earning gross monthly wages equal to at least 80 times the federal minimum wage satisfies the requirement without logging separate hours. In 2026, earning at least $580 per month fulfills the standard."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does the hold-harmless delay protect me if my income exceeds Medicaid limits?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. The hold harmless period in CMS State Medicaid Director Letter SMD# 26-006 applies only to community engagement. States may not suspend denials or disenrollments for other eligibility factors, such as age or household income."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I confirm whether my state opted into the hold-harmless extension?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Check with your state Medicaid agency. Oregon is the one state this page verified from a primary source as having elected the hold harmless period through June 30, 2027, and other states may also elect it."}}]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Person","@id":"https://www.jonathanteplitsky.com/#person","name":"Jonathan Teplitsky","honorificSuffix":"MBA","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/about/jonathan-teplitsky/","image":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/images/jonathan-teplitsky.png","jobTitle":"Publisher & Editorial Reviewer","description":"Jonathan Teplitsky is the Publisher and Editorial Reviewer of ModernWallet and Founder & CEO of Layer3Labs. He holds an MBA from Harvard Business School (2014–2016), previously led marketing for a $200M+ business at Amazon and product launches at Uber, and served as a Former U.S. Army Captain (Military Intelligence). He oversees ModernWallet's editorial standards, formula verification rules, and primary-source review process.","sameAs":["https://www.jonathanteplitsky.com/","https://www.linkedin.com/in/jonathanteplitsky/"],"alumniOf":[{"@type":"CollegeOrUniversity","name":"Harvard Business School","sameAs":"https://www.hbs.edu/"}],"hasCredential":{"@type":"EducationalOccupationalCredential","credentialCategory":"license","name":"Licensed Florida Real Estate Agent (#3440355)","recognizedBy":{"@type":"GovernmentOrganization","name":"Florida Department of Business and Professional Regulation (DBPR)","url":"https://www.myfloridalicense.com/"}},"knowsAbout":["Personal finance","Loan amortization","Interest rates and APR","Mortgage and auto financing","Retirement and investment math","Debt payoff strategies"],"worksFor":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Layer3Labs","url":"https://www.layer3labs.io"}}</script> <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":"Medicaid Work Requirements 2027: Will You Lose Coverage?","description":"Federal Medicaid work requirements start in 2027, but states using CMS's hold-harmless period cannot cut coverage before July 1, 2027. Learn what to report.","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/guides/medicaid-work-requirements-2027-hold-harmless/","image":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/favicon.svg"},"datePublished":"2026-11-25","dateModified":"2026-11-25","author":{"@type":"Organization","name":"ModernWallet Research Desk","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/editorial-policy/"},"editor":{"@type":"Person","@id":"https://www.jonathanteplitsky.com/#person","name":"Jonathan Teplitsky","honorificSuffix":"MBA","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/about/jonathan-teplitsky/","sameAs":["https://www.jonathanteplitsky.com/","https://www.linkedin.com/in/jonathanteplitsky/"]},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"ModernWallet","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com","logo":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/favicon.svg"}},"citation":[{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"CMS Fact Sheet on Medicaid Community Engagement Interim Final Rule","url":"https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/fact-sheets/medicaid-community-engagement-requirement-certain-individuals-interim-final-rule-comment-period-cms"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"CMS State Medicaid Director Letter SMD# 26-006","url":"https://www.medicaid.gov/Federal-Policy-Guidance/Downloads/smd26006.pdf"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"Medicaid.gov Community Engagement Overview","url":"https://www.medicaid.gov/resources-for-states/working-families-tax-cut-legislation/community-engagement"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"Oregon Health Authority Hold Harmless Announcement","url":"https://www.oregon.gov/oha/ERD/Pages/OHA-to-delay-enforcement-of-new-federal-Medicaid-work-rules-to-keep-people-in-Oregon-insured-11.23.2026.aspx"}]}</script> <main class="page" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <nav class="crumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <a href="/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>ModernWallet</a> <span data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>/</span> <a href="/guides/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Guides</a> <span data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>/</span> <span class="crumb-current" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Medicaid Work Requirements 2027: Will You Lose Coverage?</span> </nav> <header class="hero" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h1 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Medicaid Work Requirements 2027: Will You Lose Coverage?</h1> <div class="byline-wrap" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr> <div class="byline" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr> <span class="by" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr> By <a href="/editorial-policy/" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>ModernWallet Research Desk</a> </span> <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>·</span> <span class="rev" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>
Reviewed by <a href="/about/jonathan-teplitsky/" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>Jonathan Teplitsky, MBA</a> </span> <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>·</span> <span class="rev" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>Updated November 25, 2026</span> <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>·</span> <a class="policy-link" href="/editorial-policy/" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>How we review</a> </div>   </div>  <p class="lede" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>You will not automatically lose Medicaid coverage on January 1, 2027, if your state elects the federal hold-harmless option or if you qualify for a statutory exemption. Under federal guidance from the Centers for Medicare &amp; Medicaid Services (CMS), states adopting the transition relief may pause denials and terminations for community engagement noncompliance through June 30, 2027.</p><p class="lede" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Beneficiaries must still complete scheduled renewals and report qualifying hours or exemption circumstances to keep their files in good standing.</p> </header> <section class="tools" aria-label="Tools for this journey" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <span class="tools-title" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Tools for this journey</span> <div class="tool-pills" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <a class="tool-pill" href="/compare/aca-vs-medicaid/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>ACA vs Medicaid Comparison<span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>→</span></a><a class="tool-pill" href="/budget/aca-subsidy-calculator/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>ACA Subsidy Calculator<span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>→</span></a> </div> </section> <nav class="benefits-toc" aria-label="On This Page" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><strong data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>On This Page</strong><ol data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><li data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><a href="#benefits-section-1" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Will You Lose Medicaid on January 1?</a></li><li data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><a href="#benefits-section-2" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Who the Rule Covers and Who Is Exempt</a></li><li data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><a href="#benefits-section-3" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>What a Hold-Harmless State Can and Cannot Do</a></li><li data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><a href="#benefits-section-4" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>State Check Table</a></li><li data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><a href="#benefits-section-5" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>What to Do Now</a></li><li data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><a href="#benefits-section-6" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Our Takeaway on the 2027 Work Requirement</a></li></ol></nav>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 id="benefits-section-1" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Will You Lose Medicaid on January 1?</h2> <p>You will not automatically lose Medicaid on January 1, 2027, for missing the community engagement benchmark, but your immediate protection depends directly on whether your state chooses to pause disenrollments. Under section 71119 of Public Law 119-21, often called the Working Families Tax Cut legislation, the federal baseline requires states to implement community engagement rules no later than January 1, 2027.</p>
<p>However, the Centers for Medicare &amp; Medicaid Services (<a href="https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/fact-sheets/medicaid-community-engagement-requirement-certain-individuals-interim-final-rule-comment-period-cms" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CMS</a>) released State Medicaid Director Letter SMD# 26-006 on November 13, 2026, granting states an optional hold-harmless transition window.</p>
<p>In a state that formally elects this hold-harmless period, the Medicaid agency can suspend all eligibility denials and disenrollments based on community engagement noncompliance through June 30, 2027. This suspension applies to initial applications submitted and redetermination renewals initiated through that mid-year date.</p>
<p>Even in participating states, administrators must still collect information and assess whether members meet the engagement rules. Enrollees must continue submitting paperwork, but coverage cannot be terminated strictly for failing the work requirement during this window.</p>
<p>In states that do not adopt the hold-harmless policy, standard enforcement procedures begin immediately at the start of 2027. If an individual in an enforcement state fails to demonstrate required hours or an exemption, the state agency cannot terminate benefits without warning.</p>
<p>Federal regulations under the CMS Interim Final Rule (IFR) require the agency to send a formal noncompliance notice. Beneficiaries then receive a window of 30 calendar days to submit proof of qualifying activities or claim an exemption before disenrollment takes effect.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 id="benefits-section-2" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Who the Rule Covers and Who Is Exempt</h2> <p>The federal community engagement mandate applies strictly to non-pregnant adults ages 19 through 64 who are enrolled in the Medicaid expansion adult group or certain section 1115 minimum essential coverage demonstrations across 43 states and the District of Columbia.</p>
<p>United States territories are not subject to the mandate, and individuals entitled to or enrolled in Medicare are excluded from these requirements. To satisfy the standard, covered adults must log at least 80 hours per month of qualifying activities or earn wages equal to at least 80 times the federal minimum wage, which equals $580 per month in 2026.</p>
<p>Qualifying activities can be combined to reach the 80-hour monthly threshold. Approved activities include work, community service, a work program, or half-time education, or a combination.</p>
<p>When checking compliance, state agencies review past records: applicants must demonstrate compliance for at least one month prior to application, while existing enrollees are checked across one or more months between redetermination cycles as determined by individual state rules.</p>
<p>Federal policy provides explicit statutory exemptions that protect vulnerable groups from disenrollment. According to official CMS documentation, you are exempt from the 80-hour monthly rule if you meet any of the following criteria:</p>
<p>* You are pregnant or postpartum.
* You are a parent or primary caretaker responsible for a child age 13 or younger, or you care for an individual with a recognized disability.
* You are a former foster youth.
* You are an American Indian or Alaska Native individual.
* You are a totally disabled veteran.
* You are deemed medically frail.
* You are in substance use disorder treatment.
* You are currently complying with existing work requirements under the Temporary Assistance for Needy Families (TANF) program or the Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP).
* You are currently incarcerated in a correctional facility.</p>
<p>If you qualify for any exemption, you must notify your state agency to ensure your administrative record is coded appropriately.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 id="benefits-section-3" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>What a Hold-Harmless State Can and Cannot Do</h2> <p>State Medicaid Director Letter SMD# 26-006 establishes clear boundaries regarding state authority during the transition. A state that elects the hold-harmless option receives federal protection against CMS enforcement actions if it chooses to halt adverse eligibility decisions.</p>
<p>Under this mechanism, the state Medicaid program can suspend both application denials and mid-year disenrollments for individuals who do not show compliance with community engagement standards. States have the flexibility to implement this relief for the entire period through June 30, 2027, or choose a shorter calendar duration that suits their technical rollout schedules.</p>
<p>Electing the hold-harmless policy does not allow a state to pause normal Medicaid verification standards. States are expressly required to build reporting mechanisms, gather activity documentation, and evaluate whether applicants qualify for exemptions.</p>
<p>States intending to use the hold-harmless option starting January 1, 2027, were asked by CMS to submit notifications by December 11, 2026, through their formal community engagement mitigation plans.</p>
<p>The hold-harmless period applies only to community engagement standards under section 1902(xx)(1) of the Social Security Act and 42 CFR 435.559. A state may not suspend denials or disenrollments for other eligibility factors, such as age or household income.</p>
<p>As verified on November 23, 2026, by the <a href="https://www.oregon.gov/oha/ERD/Pages/OHA-to-delay-enforcement-of-new-federal-Medicaid-work-rules-to-keep-people-in-Oregon-insured-11.23.2026.aspx" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Oregon Health Authority</a> (OHA), Oregon is the one state this page verified from a primary source as having elected the hold-harmless period.</p>
<p>Other states may also elect it, so check your own state Medicaid agency. OHA confirmed that no Oregon Health Plan member will face coverage denials or disenrollment for community engagement noncompliance through June 30, 2027.</p>
<p>Oregon state officials noted that more than 600,000 members received informational notices in September 2026, and internal projections estimated that up to one-third of covered adults risked losing access purely due to reporting hurdles. Implementation also occurs alongside ongoing legal developments: on October 29, 2026, the federal district court in Massachusetts v.</p>
<p>Oz partially vacated specific CMS regulatory limits regarding medical condition exemptions, which CMS formally acknowledged in footnote three of SMD# 26-006.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 id="benefits-section-4" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>State Check Table</h2> <p>The table below shows what each situation means after January 1, 2027, according to CMS and the Oregon Health Authority.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="prose-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Situation</th><th scope="col">What the state may do</th><th scope="col">What you do</th><th scope="col">Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Your state elected the hold harmless period and you cannot show 80 hours, $580 a month or an exemption</th><td>May suspend denials and disenrollments for failing to show compliance, for applications received and renewals initiated through June 30, 2027 (or a shorter state period)</td><td>Keep reporting and verifying; ask your state Medicaid agency how long its period lasts</td><td>CMS SMD# 26-006</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Your state did not elect it and you cannot show them</th><td>Sends a noncompliance notice, then you have 30 calendar days to show compliance or an exemption</td><td>Answer any notice within 30 calendar days with proof of hours, income or an exemption</td><td>CMS fact sheet</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">You meet an exemption</th><td>Must assess whether you qualify for an exclusion or exception</td><td>Report the exemption and keep records</td><td>CMS fact sheet</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">You are applying for Medicaid</th><td>Checks the requirement at application, looking back at least one month; an electing state may suspend denials for applications received through June 30, 2027</td><td>Gather proof before you apply</td><td>CMS fact sheet; SMD# 26-006</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">You live in Oregon</th><td>Oregon opted in: no loss or denial of coverage for failing the rules through June 30, 2027</td><td>Still verify requirements or exemptions; you get more time at your next renewal</td><td>Oregon Health Authority</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Ask your state Medicaid agency which row applies to you.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 id="benefits-section-5" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>What to Do Now</h2> <p>Take these steps whether or not your state has adopted the hold harmless period.</p>
<ol class="prose-list"><li>Ask your state Medicaid agency whether your state elected the hold harmless period and when it ends.</li><li>Decide how you will document compliance before your next renewal. If you work, collect pay stubs, earnings statements or tax records showing at least $580 in gross monthly income or 80 logged hours.</li><li>If you meet an exemption, gather your records now.</li><li>Watch your mail for notices. In a state that has not delayed enforcement, a noncompliance notice gives you 30 calendar days to respond.</li><li>If your income rises above Medicaid limits or you lose coverage, compare Marketplace plans. Our <a href="/compare/aca-vs-medicaid/">ACA vs Medicaid comparison</a> explains the differences, and our free <a href="/budget/aca-subsidy-calculator/">ACA subsidy calculator</a> estimates premium tax credits and your out-of-pocket cost.</li></ol> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 id="benefits-section-6" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Our Takeaway on the 2027 Work Requirement</h2> <p>For most Medicaid expansion adults ages 19 through 64, the January 1, 2027 deadline poses immediate risk only if your state declined CMS hold-harmless relief and you cannot demonstrate 80 monthly activity hours, $580 in gross earnings, or an approved exemption category.</p>
<p>In an opted-in state such as Oregon, the Medicaid agency cannot deny applications or disenroll enrollees for noncompliance before July 1, 2027, but your legal duty to report hours and complete redetermination forms remains active. This landscape would shift if CMS alters national transition guidelines, if additional states withdraw or submit mitigation plans, or if federal litigation in Massachusetts v.</p>
<p>Oz expands court-ordered injunctions against enforcement.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Frequently Asked Questions</h2> <div class="faqs" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Does missing the work requirement cause immediate cancellation on January 1?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>No. In states participating in the CMS hold-harmless transition, disenrollment for community engagement noncompliance is paused through June 30, 2027.</p><p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>In non-participating states, federal rules require the agency to send a written noncompliance notice providing 30 calendar days to submit documentation or claim an exemption before terminating coverage.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Can I combine part-time employment with school or volunteering?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Yes. Federal guidelines let you combine qualifying activities, such as work, community service, a work program or half-time education, to reach the 80-hour monthly minimum.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>What earnings level satisfies the Medicaid community engagement requirement without tracking hours?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Earning gross monthly wages equal to at least 80 times the federal minimum wage satisfies the requirement without logging separate hours. In 2026, earning at least $580 per month fulfills the standard.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Does the hold-harmless delay protect me if my income exceeds Medicaid limits?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>No. The hold harmless period in CMS State Medicaid Director Letter SMD# 26-006 applies only to community engagement.</p><p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>States may not suspend denials or disenrollments for other eligibility factors, such as age or household income.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>How do I confirm whether my state opted into the hold-harmless extension?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Check with your state Medicaid agency. Oregon is the one state this page verified from a primary source as having elected the hold harmless period through June 30, 2027, and other states may also elect it.</p> </details> </div> </section>  <section class="section sources" data-astro-cid-3ntd4h4x><h2 data-astro-cid-3ntd4h4x>Sources</h2><p class="source-note" data-astro-cid-3ntd4h4x>
